# pool.dat - additions to the USGS phreeqc.dat for pool and spa water.
#
# Loaded after phreeqc.dat, which is used unchanged. This file only adds free
# available chlorine and cyanuric acid, which phreeqc.dat does not have.
#
# They are added as two new elements so PHREEQC can never convert them by
# redox into chloride, or into CO2 and N2:
#   Fc   the chlorine atom of free available chlorine, oxidation state +1.
#        HOFc is HOCl and OFc- is OCl-. Entered in mg/L as Cl2, the way test
#        kits report free chlorine: one mole of HOCl is one mole of Cl2 of
#        available chlorine, so the input weight is 70.906 g/mol (2 x 35.453).
#   Cya  the triisocyanurate ring C3N3O3. H3Cya is cyanuric acid,
#        C3H3N3O3, 129.07 g/mol.
# Chloroisocyanurates are written in the same letters: H2FcCya is H2ClCy,
# HFcCya- is HClCy-, Fc2Cya- is Cl2Cy-, and so on.
#
# Source of every constant below:
#   O'Brien, J. E., J. C. Morris and J. N. Butler (1974). "Equilibria in
#   Aqueous Solutions of Chlorinated Isocyanurate", ch. 14 in A. J. Rubin
#   (ed.), Chemistry of Water Supply, Treatment, and Distribution. Ann Arbor
#   Science Publishers, pp. 333-358.
#   as tabulated in Wojtowicz, J. A. (2001). "Relative Bactericidal
#   Effectiveness of Hypochlorous Acid and Chloroisocyanurates", Journal of
#   the Swimming Pool and Spa Industry 2(1): 34-41, Table 2, pK1 to pK13
#   at about 25 C. pK13 (HOCl) is the value of Morris (1966) in that table.
#
#   pK1  Cl3Cy + H2O = HCl2Cy + HOCl       1.8
#   pK2  HCl2Cy = H+ + Cl2Cy-              3.75
#   pK3  HCl2Cy + H2O = H2ClCy + HOCl      2.93
#   pK4  H2ClCy = H+ + HClCy-              5.33
#   pK5  H2ClCy + H2O = H3Cy + HOCl        4.07
#   pK6  H3Cy = H+ + H2Cy-                 6.88
#   pK7  Cl2Cy- + H2O = HClCy- + HOCl      4.51
#   pK8  HClCy- = H+ + ClCy2-              10.12
#   pK9  HClCy- + H2O = H2Cy- + HOCl       5.62
#   pK10 H2Cy- = H+ + HCy2-                11.40
#   pK11 ClCy2- + H2O = HCy2- + HOCl       6.90
#   pK12 HCy2- = H+ + Cy3-                 13.5
#   pK13 HOCl = H+ + OCl-                  7.54
#
# PHREEQC defines each species from the master species H3Cya and HOFc, so
# each log_k below is a sum of the pK values above, named on its line. The
# table is over-determined: pK7, pK9 and pK11 are reached from pK2 to pK8 by
# other paths, and test/database.spec.ts checks that both paths agree to
# 0.01, the rounding of the table.
#
# Limits, stated rather than hidden:
# - The constants were measured at 24-25 C and no temperature dependence is
#   given in the source, so no -delta_h or -analytic is set and PHREEQC uses
#   them unchanged at every temperature. Carbonate, calcite and borate come
#   from phreeqc.dat and do follow temperature.
# - O'Brien measured near ionic strength 0.02. PHREEQC treats the constants as
#   thermodynamic and applies its own activity corrections.
# - Alkalinity contributions follow PHREEQC's usual rule: H3Cya and HOFc
#   carry none, so each proton removed from them adds one.

SOLUTION_MASTER_SPECIES
# element  master species  alkalinity  gfw for input  element gfw
Fc         HOFc            0           70.906         35.453
Cya        H3Cya           0           129.07         126.05

SOLUTION_SPECIES
HOFc = HOFc
	log_k 0
H3Cya = H3Cya
	log_k 0

# pK13
HOFc = OFc- + H+
	log_k -7.54

# pK6
H3Cya = H2Cya- + H+
	log_k -6.88
# pK6 + pK10
H3Cya = HCya-2 + 2H+
	log_k -18.28
# pK6 + pK10 + pK12
H3Cya = Cya-3 + 3H+
	log_k -31.78

# pK5
H3Cya + HOFc = H2FcCya + H2O
	log_k 4.07
# pK5 - pK4
H3Cya + HOFc = HFcCya- + H+ + H2O
	log_k -1.26
# pK5 - pK4 - pK8
H3Cya + HOFc = FcCya-2 + 2H+ + H2O
	log_k -11.38
# pK5 + pK3
H3Cya + 2HOFc = HFc2Cya + 2H2O
	log_k 7.00
# pK5 + pK3 - pK2
H3Cya + 2HOFc = Fc2Cya- + H+ + 2H2O
	log_k 3.25
# pK5 + pK3 + pK1
H3Cya + 3HOFc = Fc3Cya + 3H2O
	log_k 8.80

PHASES
# Used only to hold pH at a target while acid or base is added. Its saturation
# index is log a(H+), so a target of -7.5 means pH 7.5. This is the fixed-pH
# technique given with EQUILIBRIUM_PHASES in the PHREEQC version 3 manual
# (Parkhurst and Appelo 2013, USGS Techniques and Methods 6-A43).
Fix_H+
	H+ = H+
	log_k 0
